<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title><%= vuln.name %> - Vuln-World</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: 'Segoe UI', Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif; margin: 0; padding: 0; background: #f9fafb; color: #374151; line-height: 1.6; }
    header { background: #1f2937; color: #fff; padding: 15px 30px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; }
    header a { color: #9ca3af; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold; }
    header a:hover { color: white; }
    .container { max-width: 900px; margin: 40px auto; background: white; padding: 40px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.05); }
    h1 { margin-top: 0; color: #111827; }
    h2 { color: #1f2937; border-bottom: 2px solid #e5e7eb; padding-bottom: 10px; margin-top: 40px; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 18px; letter-spacing: 0.05em; }
    .badge { display: inline-block; padding: 4px 8px; background: #e5e7eb; border-radius: 12px; font-size: 12px; font-weight: bold; margin-right: 10px; color: #4b5563; }
    .btn { display: inline-block; padding: 12px 24px; background: #2563eb; color: white; text-decoration: none; border-radius: 6px; font-weight: bold; margin: 20px 0; }
    .btn:hover { background: #1d4ed8; }
    pre { background: #1f2937; color: #f8f8f2; padding: 20px; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: monospace; font-size: 14px; }
    .vuln-code { border-left: 4px solid #ef4444; }
    .sec-code { border-left: 4px solid #10b981; }
    .diagram { background: #f3f4f6; padding: 20px; font-family: monospace; white-space: pre; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid #e5e7eb; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5; }
    ul { padding-left: 20px; }
    li { margin-bottom: 8px; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <header>
    <a href="/vulnerabilities">← Back to Vulnerabilities</a>
  </header>
  <div class="container">
    <h1><%= vuln.name %></h1>
    <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
      <span class="badge"><%= vuln.category %></span>
      <span class="badge"><%= vuln.difficulty %></span>
    </div>
    
    <a href="/labs/<%= vuln.lab %>?challenge=<%= vuln.id %>" class="btn">Start Lab (<%= vuln.lab %>)</a>
    
    <h2>What Is It?</h2>
    <p>Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) is an attack that forces an authenticated user to execute unwanted actions on a web application in which they are currently logged in. The attacker tricks the victim's browser into sending a request, and since the browser automatically includes cookies, the application processes the request as if it were legitimate.</p>

    <h2>In Simple Words</h2>
    <p>Imagine you are logged into your bank. You then visit a malicious website, which secretly sends a request back to your bank saying "Transfer $500 to the attacker." Because you were still logged in, your bank blindly obeys the request.</p>

    <h2>Where Does It Appear?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>State-changing requests (like transferring money or updating an email address)</li>
      <li>Applications using cookie-based session management</li>
      <li>Endpoints lacking unique anti-CSRF tokens</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How Does It Occur?</h2>
    <div class="diagram">
[Victim]   --(Logs into Bank)--> [Bank Server]
[Bank]     --(Sets Session Cookie)--> [Victim]
[Victim]   --(Visits Evil Site)--> [Attacker Server]
[Attacker] --(Returns HTML with hidden form to Bank)--> [Victim]
[Victim]   --(Browser auto-submits form + Session Cookie)--> [Bank Server]
[Bank]     --(Processes Transfer!)--> [Database]
    </div>

    <h2>HTTP Example</h2>
    <pre>POST /transfer HTTP/1.1
Host: bank.com
Cookie: session=12345

amount=1000&to=attacker</pre>

    <h2>Burp Suite Investigation</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Find a state-changing POST/PUT request.</li>
      <li>Check if it lacks anti-CSRF tokens (like a unique, unpredictable parameter).</li>
      <li>Right-click the request in Burp and select "Engagement tools -> Generate CSRF PoC".</li>
      <li>Test the PoC HTML in a browser where you have an active session to see if the action succeeds.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How Developers Accidentally Create It</h2>
    <p>Vulnerable Code:</p>
    <pre class="vuln-code">
app.post('/transfer', (req, res) => {
  const amount = req.body.amount;
  user.balance -= amount; // No CSRF validation!
});</pre>
    <p>Secure Code:</p>
    <pre class="sec-code">
app.post('/transfer', (req, res) => {
  if (req.body.csrfToken !== req.session.csrfToken) {
    return res.status(403).send('CSRF Token Invalid');
  }
  const amount = req.body.amount;
  user.balance -= amount;
});</pre>

    <h2>Impact</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Unauthorized actions performed on behalf of users</li>
      <li>Account takeover (e.g., changing passwords or email addresses)</li>
      <li>Financial loss through fraudulent transfers</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to Find It in Real Applications</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Check all forms for hidden anti-CSRF tokens.</li>
      <li>Remove any anti-CSRF token you find and see if the request still succeeds.</li>
      <li>Check the `SameSite` attribute of session cookies.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Remediation</h2>
    <p>Use Anti-CSRF tokens for all state-changing requests. Additionally, set the `SameSite` attribute on session cookies to `Lax` or `Strict` to prevent browsers from sending cookies in cross-site requests.</p>

    <h2>Secure Design Checklist</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Implement anti-CSRF tokens (Synchronizer Token Pattern).</li>
      <li>Use `SameSite=Lax` or `SameSite=Strict` for cookies.</li>
      <li>Require re-authentication for highly sensitive actions (like password changes).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Lab Investigation Guide</h2>
    <p>In this lab, the transfer funds function does not verify if the request was intentionally made by the user on the site. Your goal is to create a malicious HTML file that, when opened, automatically sends a transfer request to the application on your behalf.</p>

    <h2>Post-Lab Explanation</h2>
    <p>You successfully executed a CSRF attack. By constructing a hidden form and using JavaScript to auto-submit it, you forced the browser to make a cross-origin request to the transfer endpoint. Since the application lacked anti-CSRF tokens, it couldn't distinguish your forged request from a legitimate one.</p>
  </div>
</body>
</html>
